{"questions":[{"question_id":"1ky7cst0b","paperTitle":"JEE Advanced 2014 Paper 1 Offline","year":2014,"examDate":2014,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"redox-reactions","chapterGroup":"physical-chemistry","topic":"oxidation-and-reduction-reactions","type":"integer","marks":3,"negMarks":0,"permalink":"consider-the-following-list-of-reagents-acidified-ksub2-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-ak3i1jos0kcj6atp","content":"Consider the following list of reagents, acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>, alkaline KMnO<sub>4</sub>, CuSO<sub>4</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, Cl<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>3</sub>, FeCl<sub>3</sub>, HNO<sub>3</sub> and Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>. The total number of reagents that can oxidise aqueous iodide to iodine is","options":[],"correct_options":[],"explanation":"Acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>, CuSO<sub>4</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>, Cl<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>3</sub>, FeCl<sub>3</sub> and HNO<sub>3</sub> oxidise aq. iodide to iodine. Alkaline KMnO<sub>4</sub> oxidise aq. iodide to IO<mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.706ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.432ex\" height=\"2.57ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -823.8 633.1 1136\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msubsup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(33,411.6) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(33,-296.6) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container>.<br><br>Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> is a strong reducing agent which on reaction with I<sub>2</sub> produces I<sup><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></sup>.<br><br>Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> + I<sub>2</sub> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> 2I<sup><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.186ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.505ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -583 778 665\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></sup> + Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>4</sub>O<sub>6</sub><br><br>Therefore, no reaction takes place between Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> and iodide ion. Hence, correct integer is (7)."}]}